  <title>5.13.2: Creating Exponential Functions from Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01481</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.13.2: Creating Exponential Functions from Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>46990163-00b1-4989-bb07-4275aa15a88a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity </title>
      <para>Examine the graph representing an exponential function \(f\). The function \(f\) gives the value of a computer, in
  dollars, as a function of time, \(x\), measured in years since the time of purchase.</para>
      <para>
        
          <media alt="GRAPH OF AN EXPONENTIAL DECAY FUNCTION, WHERE VALUE IN DOLLARS IS A FUNCTION OF TIME. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS 0 COMMA FOUR HUNDRED, 2 COMMA 100, AND 4 COMMA 25.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_03_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para>Based on the graph, what can you say about the following?</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> The purchase price of the computer</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>$400</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>The value of \(f\) when \(x\) is 1</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(1)=200\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para> The meaning of \(f(1)\)</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>The value of the computer after 1 year</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para> How the value of the computer is changing each year</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>The computer loses half of its value each year.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>An equation that defines \(f\). (If needed, use the '^' symbol to enter an exponent.)</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=400(\frac{1}{2})^x\) or equivalent (i.e., \(f(x)=400 \cdot (0.5)^x\)).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Whether the value of \(f\) will reach 0 after 10 years</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>No, it will not. After 10 years, the value of the computer will be a little less than $0.40.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para>For questions 7 - 10, examine the graphs of the exponential functions.</para>
      <para>Examine the graph of the exponential function below.
  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_03_02.jpg"/></media></para>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para> Write an equation that defines the function. Use the “^” symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para> \(f(x)=5\cdot 3^x\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>For each, write an equation that defines the function and find the value of the function when \(x\) is 5.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para> \(f(5)=1,215\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para>Examine the graph of the exponential function below. <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_03_03.jpg"/></media></para>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem><para> Write an equation that defines the function. Use the “^” symbol to enter an exponent.</para><para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>compare your work.
  compare your work.
  </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para> \(f(x)=20 \cdot (\frac{1}{2})^x\) (or equivalent, i.e., \(f(x)=20 \cdot (0.5)^x\)).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>For each, write an equation that defines the function and find the value of the function when \(x\) is 5.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para> \(f(5)=0.625\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <title> Video: Interpreting an Exponential Function from a Graph</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about interpreting exponential functions:</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/caccc8b7929b24bca06458ed8e8c609a93390e10"/>
      </media>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            
            
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <title> Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para>Consider a function \(f\) defined by \(f(x)=a \cdot b^x\).</para>
          <para>If the graph of \(f\) goes through the points \((2,10)\) and \((8,30)\), would you expect \(f(5)\) to be less
      than, equal to, or greater than 20?</para>
          <para>If the graph of \(f\) goes through the points \((2,30)\) and \((8,10)\), would you expect \(f(5)\) to be less
      than, equal to, or greater than 20?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The value 20 is what you would get for \(f(5)\) if \(f\) were a linear function through \((2,10)\) and \((8,30)\)
      (or \((2,30)\) and \((8,10)\)). Looking at graphs representing our exponential functions, we can see that the line
      connecting two points on the graph always seems to be above the graph of the exponential function itself. Thus, we
      would expect \(f(5)&lt;20\) in both cases.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check">
      <para>Which of the following is the function for the curve graphed below?</para>
      <para>
        
          <media alt="GRAPH OF AN EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTION. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS 0 COMMA 100, 1 COMMA THREE HUNDRED, AND 2 COMMA 900.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_04_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
    </note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section>
      <title> Writing Functions from Graphs</title>
      <para>
          <media alt="GRAPH OF AN EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTION. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS 0 COMMA 4, 1 COMMA 12, AND 2 COMMA 36.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para>Find the equation of the function graphed above. Then find the value at \(f(5)\).</para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
                <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                  <item>Find the value of \(a\), the starting point.</item>
                </list>
             </emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
        \(a=4\)
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
                <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                  <item>Find the \(b\), the growth factor.</item>
                </list>
             </emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
        \(b=3\)
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
                <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                  <item>Write the equation of the function.</item>
                </list>
             </emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
        \(f(x)=4 \cdot 3^x\)
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
                <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                  <item>Find \(f(5)\).</item>
                </list>
             </emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
        \(f(5)=4 \cdot 3^5=972\)
      </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <title> Try It: Writing Functions from Graphs</title>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Use the graph below to write the equation of the function. Then find \(f(5)\).</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="GRAPH OF AN EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTION. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS 0 COMMA 2, 1 COMMA 8, AND 2 COMMA 32.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_05_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answers:</para>
            <para>Here is how to write the exponential function from the graph:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
                      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                        <item>Find the value of \(a\), the starting point.</item>
                      </list>
                   </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          \(a=2\)
        </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
                      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                        <item>Find the \(b\), the growth factor.</item>
                      </list>
                   </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          \(b=4\)
        </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
                      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                        <item>Write the equation of the function.</item>
                      </list>
                   </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          \(f(x)=2 \cdot 4^x\)
        </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
                      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                        <item>Find \(f(5)\).</item>
                      </list>
                   </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          \(f(x)=2 \cdot 4^5=2048\)
        </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
    </section>
    
